WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:04.826
Six Moves, from EL Publishing. Move three: unpack one sentence.

2
00:00:05.276 --> 00:00:09.587
Your Progressing students, the level threes and fours, are fine in discussion.

3
00:00:09.587 --> 00:00:15.225
Then you assign four pages of the textbook and they come back with nothing. The page didn't beat them.

4
00:00:15.225 --> 00:00:16.496
One sentence on it did.

5
00:00:16.946 --> 00:00:18.071
Here's a real one.

6
00:00:18.071 --> 00:00:25.694
During photosynthesis, energy from sunlight is used by plants to convert carbon dioxide and water into glucose and oxygen.

7
00:00:25.694 --> 00:00:31.568
One sentence. Five things happening. Passive voice. The subject, plants, buried in the middle.

8
00:00:32.018 --> 00:00:37.476
The move: pick that sentence on purpose, before class, and take it apart on the board with the students.

9
00:00:37.476 --> 00:00:39.155
Ask the questions a reader asks.

10
00:00:39.605 --> 00:00:44.569
Who does it? Plants. Not energy — energy is used by plants. Plants do it.

11
00:00:45.019 --> 00:00:48.165
Does what? Convert. Change one thing into another.

12
00:00:48.615 --> 00:00:53.317
Converts what into what? Carbon dioxide and water, into glucose and oxygen.

13
00:00:53.767 --> 00:00:57.885
Using what? Energy from sunlight. Now every piece has a job.

14
00:00:58.335 --> 00:01:03.452
Then rewrite it together as three short sentences. Plants use energy from sunlight.

15
00:01:03.452 --> 00:01:08.506
They convert carbon dioxide and water. They make glucose and oxygen. Same science.

16
00:01:08.506 --> 00:01:13.191
Three sentences a level two can read and a level four can now see inside of.

17
00:01:13.641 --> 00:01:15.060
That's three minutes of class.

18
00:01:15.060 --> 00:01:22.249
And you didn't just help the English learners — you taught every student in the room how to read a science sentence, which nobody else is teaching them.

19
00:01:22.700 --> 00:01:27.712
Make it a habit. One sentence a day — the one you know will stop them. Pick it before class.

20
00:01:27.712 --> 00:01:32.832
Ask the same four questions every time: who, does what, to what, using what. Rewrite it short.

21
00:01:32.832 --> 00:01:37.463
After two weeks, hand the next sentence to a pair of students and let them unpack it.

22
00:01:37.913 --> 00:01:41.837
Tomorrow: open next week's reading and find the sentence.

23
00:01:41.837 --> 00:01:48.722
Six Moves is part of the PD Library at radan55 dot github dot io slash everyteacher. Free, no login.
